WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:19.400
<v Facilitator>Thanks for making the time, both of you. The question on the table is the zoning change on Ridge Road. I'd like each of you to say where you're starting from, and then we'll go from there. Dana, do you want to open?

2
00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:41.880
<v Dana>Sure. I've lived on Ridge for nineteen years. The proposal puts four-story buildings across from my kitchen window. And I want to be honest, when I first read about it I was angry, because nobody from the city called anyone on my street. We found out from a flyer.

3
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:58.640
<v Marcus>Can I just say, that part I completely agree with. The outreach was bad. I'm on the other side of this and I still think the way they handled notification was disrespectful to the people who actually live there.

4
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:14.220
<v Dana>Okay. That's not what I expected you to say.

5
00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:39.500
<v Marcus>Well, I'm not going to defend a bad process just because I like the outcome. Where I come out differently is, my sister has been looking for an apartment in this district for two years. She's a nurse at the hospital six blocks from Ridge. She can't live near where she works. That's the thing I can't get past.

6
00:01:40.100 --> 00:02:02.330
<v Dana>I hear that. I do. My son is in the same spot, honestly, he's twenty-six and he's still in our basement. So I'm not sitting here saying nobody should be able to move in. It's more, why is it always our street. Why is it never the streets with the bigger houses.

7
00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:18.770
<v Marcus>That's a fair question and I don't have a good answer to it. It does tend to land on the blocks with the least political pull. That's true and it's not right.

8
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:38.400
<v Facilitator>Marcus, can you say more about what your sister's search has actually looked like? Dana, I'll come back to you after.

9
00:02:38.900 --> 00:03:09.100
<v Marcus>She's looked at maybe forty places. The ones she can afford are a forty minute drive out, which means she's driving home at two in the morning after a shift. Twice she's fallen asleep at a light. That's the part that gets me. It's not abstract for me, it's my sister on the road at two in the morning.

10
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:34.200
<v Dana>I didn't know that. That's, yeah. That's different from how this gets talked about at the meetings, where it's all numbers and units. Look, I'm not going to say I've changed my mind on four stories. I haven't. But I think I've been treating this like it's developers versus us, and your sister isn't a developer.

11
00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:52.100
<v Marcus>And I've been treating your side like it's all people who just don't want change. Which, hearing you on the notification thing, that's not what's going on with you.

12
00:03:52.700 --> 00:04:11.900
<v Dana>What would it take for you, would you be okay with three stories instead of four? I'm asking honestly, I don't know if that's a real thing or if it breaks the math.

13
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:38.200
<v Marcus>I genuinely don't know. I think it changes the unit count enough that it might not pencil out for anyone building it, but I'd want to actually look rather than guess. If it did work, I'd take it. I care about my sister having somewhere to live, I don't care about the fourth floor.

14
00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:51.300
<v Facilitator>That's a good place to pause. We're at time. Same room in two weeks.
